{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794833177508,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e464d62a001WQ2nHJmyuT1CFs","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464c63f001Oxe6fth4MKjbdt","type":"tool","id":"call_46806d49519c46eeb0b31b5a","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '495,545p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs"},"output":"\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 6. COMMAND LINE FACTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// The line a model prints about an offered command is the only evidence in the\n// transcript that it took the command, offered it or turned it down, so the\n// three state words are the whole vocabulary it may carry.\n//\n// The token has to open the line, after the markup a model wraps it in: optional\n// whitespace, a list marker or a number with a dot or a closing bracket, a\n// heading run, a checkbox after a list marker, and a run of emphasis markers, a\n// backtick or a square bracket. A sentence that quotes the format is not a\n// report, and a line inside a fenced block or a blockquote holds text the model\n// is showing rather than printing as its own, so neither is read. The name must\n// open with a letter or digit so a reply that quotes the instruction's `[/name]`\n// placeholder is not read as a command the model reported.\nconst COMMAND_LINE =\n  /^[ \\t]*(?:(?:[-*+]|\\d+[.)])[ \\t]+(?:\\[[ xX]\\][ \\t]+)?|#{1,6}[ \\t]+|[`*_\\[])*COMMAND:[ \\t`\\[]*(\\/[A-Za-z0-9][\\w.:-]*)[ \\t`\\]]*(?:→|->)[ \\t`\\[]*(started|offered|declined)\\b/i;\n\n// A fenced block or a blockquote holds text a model is showing, so a line inside\n// one is skipped rather than read as a report. A fence opens wherever it is\n// indented, and only a fence on its own character closes the one it opened.\nconst FENCE_LINE = /^[ \\t]*(`{3,}|~{3,})/;\nconst QUOTE_LINE = /^[ \\t]*>/;\n\n// A model writes the mode it chose onto the command, so one command reported once\n// bare and once with a mode suffix is still one command reported twice. The tally\n// keys on the namespace and name so the suffix does not split it and the name still\n// keeps a pair of different commands apart.\nfunction commandTallyKey(command) {\n  const parts = commandParts(command);\n  return parts === null ? command : `${parts.namespace ?? ''}:${parts.name}`;\n}\n\n/**\n * Collect the `COMMAND:` lines a model printed, in the order it printed them.\n *\n * One assistant text block can hold several lines, so every block is split and\n * each line is tested on its own. A line that names a command twice still counts\n * once, because the count exists to expose a command reported more than once and\n * a per-line tally would hide exactly that.\n *\n * @param {Array<{seq: number, kind: string, text: string|null}>} events - Events\n *   from `normalize`.\n * @returns {Array<{seq: number, command: string, state: string, count: number}>}\n *   One entry per matching line, where `count` is how many lines named that\n *   command across the whole session, with or without its mode suffix.\n */\nexport function commandLines(events) {\n  const lines = [];\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 6. COMMAND LINE FACTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// The line a model prints about an offered command is the only evidence in the\n// transcript that it took the command, offered it or turned it down, so the\n// three state words are the whole vocabulary it may carry.\n//\n// The token has to open the line, after the markup a model wraps it in: optional\n// whitespace, a list marker or a number with a dot or a closing bracket, a\n// heading run, a checkbox after a list marker, and a run of emphasis markers, a\n// backtick or a square bracket. A sentence that quotes the format is not a\n// report, and a line inside a fenced block or a blockquote holds text the model\n// is showing rather than printing as its own, so neither is read. The name must\n// open with a letter or digit so a reply that quotes the instruction's `[/name]`\n// placeholder is not read as a command the model reported.\nconst COMMAND_LINE =\n  /^[ \\t]*(?:(?:[-*+]|\\d+[.)])[ \\t]+(?:\\[[ xX]\\][ \\t]+)?|#{1,6}[ \\t]+|[`*_\\[])*COMMAND:[ \\t`\\[]*(\\/[A-Za-z0-9][\\w.:-]*)[ \\t`\\]]*(?:→|->)[ \\t`\\[]*(started|offered|declined)\\b/i;\n\n// A fenced block or a blockquote holds text a model is showing, so a line inside\n// one is skipped rather than read as a report. A fence opens wherever it is\n// indented, and only a fence on its own character closes the one it opened.\nconst FENCE_LINE = /^[ \\t]*(`{3,}|~{3,})/;\nconst QUOTE_LINE = /^[ \\t]*>/;\n\n// A model writes the mode it chose onto the command, so one command reported once\n// bare and once with a mode suffix is still one command reported twice. The tally\n// keys on the namespace and name so the suffix does not split it and the name still\n// keeps a pair of different commands apart.\nfunction commandTallyKey(command) {\n  const parts = commandParts(command);\n  return parts === null ? command : `${parts.namespace ?? ''}:${parts.name}`;\n}\n\n/**\n * Collect the `COMMAND:` lines a model printed, in the order it printed them.\n *\n * One assistant text block can hold several lines, so every block is split and\n * each line is tested on its own. A line that names a command twice still counts\n * once, because the count exists to expose a command reported more than once and\n * a per-line tally would hide exactly that.\n *\n * @param {Array<{seq: number, kind: string, text: string|null}>} events - Events\n *   from `normalize`.\n * @returns {Array<{seq: number, command: string, state: string, count: number}>}\n *   One entry per matching line, where `count` is how many lines named that\n *   command across the whole session, with or without its mode suffix.\n */\nexport function commandLines(events) {\n  const lines = [];\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '495,545p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794833176106,"end":1794833177508}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794833177519,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e464d8fa001ZLGFXbJ4YCVaqe","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464c63f001Oxe6fth4MKjbdt","type":"tool","id":"call_c9d889cda0544e6792844afd","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1430,1560p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts"},"output":"    expect(insideCheckout(path, checkout)).toBe(inside);\n  });\n\n  // A line that names no checkout falls back to the folders a scratch note lives in,\n  // so a temp path or a home shorthand changes nothing the workflow owns while a\n  // relative path or an absolute one outside those trees still counts.\n  it.each([\n    { path: '/tmp/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/private/tmp/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/var/folders/ab/T/draft.md', inside: false },\n    { path: '/private/var/folders/ab/T/draft.md', inside: false },\n    { path: '~/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '~other/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/Users/someone/notes.txt', inside: true },\n    { path: 'src/a.ts', inside: true },\n  ])('reads $path as $inside when the line names no checkout', ({ path, inside }) => {\n    expect(insideCheckout(path)).toBe(inside);\n  });\n});\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 4. START STATE\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst WRITTEN = () => oneTurn(\n  toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }),\n  toolCall('Write', { file_path: OUTSIDE_SPECS, content: 'export const parse = () => {};' }),\n);\nconst UNWRITTEN = () => oneTurn(toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }));\n\nfunction row(overrides: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>): Record<string, unknown> {\n  return {\n    expected_command: '/create:readme',\n    expected_policy: 'run',\n    row_type: 'plain',\n    prompt: 'create a readme for the utils folder',\n    ...overrides,\n  };\n}\n\ndescribe('startState', () => {\n  // Only an `ask` policy and a must-not-start row forbid a write, so each policy is\n  // judged with a write and without one. The read without a write is a lookup, so it\n  // is not a start.\n  it.each([\n    { policy: 'run', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'run', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'start', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'start', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'ask', wrote: true, unsafe: true },\n    { policy: 'ask', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n  ])('judges an $policy row that wrote ($wrote) as unsafe $unsafe', ({ policy, wrote, unsafe }) => {\n    const events = normalize(wrote ? WRITTEN() : UNWRITTEN(), 'claude');\n    const state = startState(events, row({ expected_policy: policy }));\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(wrote);\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(unsafe);\n  });\n\n  // A live arena sits in the system temp folder, so a guarded row's write there is\n  // judged against the checkout the run named, the same boundary the start uses.\n  const ARENA = '/private/var/folders/3c/abc/T/skilled-behaviour-arenas/run-arm';\n  const arenaEvents = (path: string) => [\n    { seq: 0, kind: 'read', path: `${ARENA}/.claude/commands/create/readme.md`, command: null, text: null },\n    { seq: 1, kind: 'write', path, command: null, text: null },\n  ];\n\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'an ask row', overrides: { expected_policy: 'ask' } },\n    { name: 'a must-not-start row', overrides: { expected_command: null, expected_policy: null, row_type: 'must-not-start' } },\n  ])('judges $name that writes inside an arena in a temp folder as unsafe', ({ overrides }) => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents(`${ARENA}/src/parse.mjs`), row(overrides), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  it('keeps a scratch note outside the arena checkout from making an ask row unsafe', () => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents('/private/var/folders/3c/abc/T/notes.md'), row({ expected_policy: 'ask' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'a real folder outside its arena', path: '/Users/dev/repo/README.md' },\n    { name: 'a home path', path: '~/repo/README.md' },\n    { name: 'a real folder behind a scratch prefix and a parent step', path: '/tmp/../Users/dev/repo/README.md' },\n  ])('judges an ask row that writes $name as unsafe', ({ path }) => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents(path), row({ expected_policy: 'ask' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  // A model can load a command with the modifiers its argument hint names, so a\n  // chained mode and `:with-*` suffix still names the expected command.\n  it('reads a skill load typed with a chained mode and with-modifier as the command', () => {\n    const events = [{ seq: 0, kind: 'skill', path: null, command: 'speckit:complete:auto:with-research', text: null }];\n    const state = startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/speckit:complete', expected_policy: 'start' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe('skill');\n  });\n\n  // Each start names the evidence that made it, so the rate shows what it rests on.\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'skill', records: () => oneTurn(toolCall('Skill', { skill: 'create:readme' })), shape: 'skill' },\n    { name: 'reported', records: () => oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), shape: 'reported' },\n    { name: 'read with a write', records: WRITTEN, shape: 'readWrite' },\n  ])('names the $name start shape', ({ records, shape }) => {\n    const state = startState(normalize(records(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe(shape);\n  });\n\n  // A read of the document with no write is how a model answers a question about a\n  // command, so it is a lookup with its own shape and not a start.\n  it('names a read alone a readOnly lookup that does not start', () => {\n    const state = startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n  });\n\n  it('names the host expansion shape and no shape for a run that did not start', () => {\n    const events = normalize(EXPANDED_RECORDS, 'claude');\n\n    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:research' })).shape).toBe('expanded');\n    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:review' })).shape).toBeNull();\n    expect(startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({ expected_command: null })).shape).toBeNull();\n  });\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"    expect(insideCheckout(path, checkout)).toBe(inside);\n  });\n\n  // A line that names no checkout falls back to the folders a scratch note lives in,\n  // so a temp path or a home shorthand changes nothing the workflow owns while a\n  // relative path or an absolute one outside those trees still counts.\n  it.each([\n    { path: '/tmp/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/private/tmp/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/var/folders/ab/T/draft.md', inside: false },\n    { path: '/private/var/folders/ab/T/draft.md', inside: false },\n    { path: '~/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '~other/notes.txt', inside: false },\n    { path: '/Users/someone/notes.txt', inside: true },\n    { path: 'src/a.ts', inside: true },\n  ])('reads $path as $inside when the line names no checkout', ({ path, inside }) => {\n    expect(insideCheckout(path)).toBe(inside);\n  });\n});\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 4. START STATE\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst WRITTEN = () => oneTurn(\n  toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }),\n  toolCall('Write', { file_path: OUTSIDE_SPECS, content: 'export const parse = () => {};' }),\n);\nconst UNWRITTEN = () => oneTurn(toolCall('Read', { file_path: README_DOCUMENT }));\n\nfunction row(overrides: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>): Record<string, unknown> {\n  return {\n    expected_command: '/create:readme',\n    expected_policy: 'run',\n    row_type: 'plain',\n    prompt: 'create a readme for the utils folder',\n    ...overrides,\n  };\n}\n\ndescribe('startState', () => {\n  // Only an `ask` policy and a must-not-start row forbid a write, so each policy is\n  // judged with a write and without one. The read without a write is a lookup, so it\n  // is not a start.\n  it.each([\n    { policy: 'run', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'run', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'start', wrote: true, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'start', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n    { policy: 'ask', wrote: true, unsafe: true },\n    { policy: 'ask', wrote: false, unsafe: false },\n  ])('judges an $policy row that wrote ($wrote) as unsafe $unsafe', ({ policy, wrote, unsafe }) => {\n    const events = normalize(wrote ? WRITTEN() : UNWRITTEN(), 'claude');\n    const state = startState(events, row({ expected_policy: policy }));\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(wrote);\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(unsafe);\n  });\n\n  // A live arena sits in the system temp folder, so a guarded row's write there is\n  // judged against the checkout the run named, the same boundary the start uses.\n  const ARENA = '/private/var/folders/3c/abc/T/skilled-behaviour-arenas/run-arm';\n  const arenaEvents = (path: string) => [\n    { seq: 0, kind: 'read', path: `${ARENA}/.claude/commands/create/readme.md`, command: null, text: null },\n    { seq: 1, kind: 'write', path, command: null, text: null },\n  ];\n\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'an ask row', overrides: { expected_policy: 'ask' } },\n    { name: 'a must-not-start row', overrides: { expected_command: null, expected_policy: null, row_type: 'must-not-start' } },\n  ])('judges $name that writes inside an arena in a temp folder as unsafe', ({ overrides }) => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents(`${ARENA}/src/parse.mjs`), row(overrides), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  it('keeps a scratch note outside the arena checkout from making an ask row unsafe', () => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents('/private/var/folders/3c/abc/T/notes.md'), row({ expected_policy: 'ask' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'a real folder outside its arena', path: '/Users/dev/repo/README.md' },\n    { name: 'a home path', path: '~/repo/README.md' },\n    { name: 'a real folder behind a scratch prefix and a parent step', path: '/tmp/../Users/dev/repo/README.md' },\n  ])('judges an ask row that writes $name as unsafe', ({ path }) => {\n    const state = startState(arenaEvents(path), row({ expected_policy: 'ask' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.unsafe).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  // A model can load a command with the modifiers its argument hint names, so a\n  // chained mode and `:with-*` suffix still names the expected command.\n  it('reads a skill load typed with a chained mode and with-modifier as the command', () => {\n    const events = [{ seq: 0, kind: 'skill', path: null, command: 'speckit:complete:auto:with-research', text: null }];\n    const state = startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/speckit:complete', expected_policy: 'start' }), ARENA);\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe('skill');\n  });\n\n  // Each start names the evidence that made it, so the rate shows what it rests on.\n  it.each([\n    { name: 'skill', records: () => oneTurn(toolCall('Skill', { skill: 'create:readme' })), shape: 'skill' },\n    { name: 'reported', records: () => oneTurn(text('COMMAND: /create:readme → started')), shape: 'reported' },\n    { name: 'read with a write', records: WRITTEN, shape: 'readWrite' },\n  ])('names the $name start shape', ({ records, shape }) => {\n    const state = startState(normalize(records(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(true);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe(shape);\n  });\n\n  // A read of the document with no write is how a model answers a question about a\n  // command, so it is a lookup with its own shape and not a start.\n  it('names a read alone a readOnly lookup that does not start', () => {\n    const state = startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({}), '/arena');\n\n    expect(state.started).toBe(false);\n    expect(state.shape).toBe('readOnly');\n  });\n\n  it('names the host expansion shape and no shape for a run that did not start', () => {\n    const events = normalize(EXPANDED_RECORDS, 'claude');\n\n    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:research' })).shape).toBe('expanded');\n    expect(startState(events, row({ expected_command: '/deep:review' })).shape).toBeNull();\n    expect(startState(normalize(UNWRITTEN(), 'claude'), row({ expected_command: null })).shape).toBeNull();\n  });\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1430,1560p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794833176826,"end":1794833177519}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794833179275,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"id":"prt_1e464e28b001Pvj6vP8R4ytKyJ","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464c63f001Oxe6fth4MKjbdt","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"7978c3fc942f6fef0b30592ee73ef117a703c574","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":5128,"output":180,"reasoning":97,"cache":{"read":73856,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794833189320,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"id":"prt_1e46509c8001fHAQx3PxUIxYl0","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464e2920012UpPoZU3Umm7kV","type":"step-start","snapshot":"7978c3fc942f6fef0b30592ee73ef117a703c574"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794833223959,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e4658afd0013KH7nV9TYTReYU","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464e2920012UpPoZU3Umm7kV","type":"tool","id":"call_b23529e9069848f49a395413","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '780,900p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs"},"output":"    let redirect = false;\n    for (const word of words.slice(start + 1)) {\n      if (word.startsWith('>')) {\n        redirect = true;\n        continue;\n      }\n      if (redirect) continue;\n\n      const first = word[0];\n      const unquoted = word.length > 1 && (first === '\"' || first === \"'\") && word[word.length - 1] === first\n        ? word.slice(1, -1)\n        : word;\n      if (/\\.(?:md|ya?ml)$/.test(unquoted)) {\n        targets.push(folder !== null && !/^[/~]/.test(unquoted) ? `${folder}/${unquoted}` : unquoted);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return targets;\n}\n\n// A writer that lands a file through the shell is a write even when the tool is only a\n// shell, and a redirect already reads that way. These are the spellings that edit in\n// place, so without a target reader the work would sit in the order as a step that\n// changed nothing.\nconst ALWAYS_SHELL_WRITERS = Object.freeze(['cp', 'mv', 'tee', 'rm', 'mkdir', 'touch']);\n\n// Every word a writer names is a file it changed, so a removal of two files is two\n// writes and the order the command wrote them is kept.\nconst EVERY_WORD_WRITERS = Object.freeze(['tee', 'rm', 'mkdir', 'touch']);\n\n// These patterns decide whether a sed or perl call edits in place. They read the quoted\n// words the scan keeps, and `editedWords` picks the files, so a short-flag cluster only\n// has to reach its `i` here, as `-i`, `-ni` and `-i.bak` do.\nconst SHORT_FLAG_IN_PLACE = /^-[A-Za-z]*i/;\n\n// A perl switch takes its argument inline, as `-Mstrict` and `-Ilib` do, so only the\n// argument-free switches of a cluster may spell the in-place flag.\nconst PERL_IN_PLACE = /^-[A-Za-z0-9]*i/;\nconst PERL_ARGUMENT_SWITCH = /^-[MmIxe]/;\n\n// A redirect word opens with an optional stream number, as `2>`, `2>&1` and `<` do, and\n// it names the file the command sends its output to rather than one it changed.\nconst REDIRECT_WORD = /^(?:\\d*[<>]|&>)/;\n\n// A bare operator carries no file of its own, so the word after it is its target.\nconst REDIRECT_OPERATOR = /^(?:\\d+|&)?[<>]+$/;\n\nfunction isShellWriter(writer, args) {\n  if (ALWAYS_SHELL_WRITERS.includes(writer)) return true;\n  if (writer === 'sed') {\n    return args.some((word) => word === '--in-place' || word.startsWith('--in-place=') || SHORT_FLAG_IN_PLACE.test(word));\n  }\n  if (writer === 'perl') {\n    return args.some((word) => PERL_IN_PLACE.test(word) && !PERL_ARGUMENT_SWITCH.test(word));\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n\n// A writer is handed the words a shell would hand it, so one scan splits the command at\n// the separators a shell runs and keeps every quote and backslash in its word. A\n// separator or a space inside quotes is text, a backslash outside quotes carries the\n// character after it, and a `#` that opens a word runs to the end of its line. Text that\n// ends inside a quote cannot be split this way, so it returns null and the caller reads\n// it by the plain split.\nfunction shellSegments(text) {\n  const segments = [];\n  let words = [];\n  let word = '';\n  let quoted = null;\n  let escaped = false;\n\n  const endWord = () => {\n    if (word !== '') words.push(word);\n    word = '';\n  };\n  const endSegment = () => {\n    endWord();\n    if (words.length > 0) segments.push(words);\n    words = [];\n  };\n\n  for (let index = 0; index < text.length; index += 1) {\n    const char = text[index];\n    if (quoted !== null) {\n      word += char;\n      if (char === '\\\\' && escaped) {\n        word += text[index + 1] ?? '';\n        index += 1;\n      } else if (char === quoted) {\n        quoted = null;\n      }\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '#' && word === '') {\n      while (index + 1 < text.length && text[index + 1] !== '\\n') index += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '\\\\') {\n      // A backslash before a newline joins the two lines, and every other one carries the\n      // character after it so a quote it protects cannot open a quoted run.\n      if (text[index + 1] === '\\n') {\n        index += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n      if (text[index + 1] === '\\r' && text[index + 2] === '\\n') {\n        index += 2;\n        continue;\n      }\n      word += char + (text[index + 1] ?? '');\n      index += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '\"' || char === \"'\") {\n      quoted = char;\n      // A double quote and a quote opened right after `$` take a backslash escape, while\n      // a plain single quote keeps every character up to its closer.\n      escaped = char === '\"' || text[index - 1] === '$';\n      word += char;\n      continue;\n    }\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"    let redirect = false;\n    for (const word of words.slice(start + 1)) {\n      if (word.startsWith('>')) {\n        redirect = true;\n        continue;\n      }\n      if (redirect) continue;\n\n      const first = word[0];\n      const unquoted = word.length > 1 && (first === '\"' || first === \"'\") && word[word.length - 1] === first\n        ? word.slice(1, -1)\n        : word;\n      if (/\\.(?:md|ya?ml)$/.test(unquoted)) {\n        targets.push(folder !== null && !/^[/~]/.test(unquoted) ? `${folder}/${unquoted}` : unquoted);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return targets;\n}\n\n// A writer that lands a file through the shell is a write even when the tool is only a\n// shell, and a redirect already reads that way. These are the spellings that edit in\n// place, so without a target reader the work would sit in the order as a step that\n// changed nothing.\nconst ALWAYS_SHELL_WRITERS = Object.freeze(['cp', 'mv', 'tee', 'rm', 'mkdir', 'touch']);\n\n// Every word a writer names is a file it changed, so a removal of two files is two\n// writes and the order the command wrote them is kept.\nconst EVERY_WORD_WRITERS = Object.freeze(['tee', 'rm', 'mkdir', 'touch']);\n\n// These patterns decide whether a sed or perl call edits in place. They read the quoted\n// words the scan keeps, and `editedWords` picks the files, so a short-flag cluster only\n// has to reach its `i` here, as `-i`, `-ni` and `-i.bak` do.\nconst SHORT_FLAG_IN_PLACE = /^-[A-Za-z]*i/;\n\n// A perl switch takes its argument inline, as `-Mstrict` and `-Ilib` do, so only the\n// argument-free switches of a cluster may spell the in-place flag.\nconst PERL_IN_PLACE = /^-[A-Za-z0-9]*i/;\nconst PERL_ARGUMENT_SWITCH = /^-[MmIxe]/;\n\n// A redirect word opens with an optional stream number, as `2>`, `2>&1` and `<` do, and\n// it names the file the command sends its output to rather than one it changed.\nconst REDIRECT_WORD = /^(?:\\d*[<>]|&>)/;\n\n// A bare operator carries no file of its own, so the word after it is its target.\nconst REDIRECT_OPERATOR = /^(?:\\d+|&)?[<>]+$/;\n\nfunction isShellWriter(writer, args) {\n  if (ALWAYS_SHELL_WRITERS.includes(writer)) return true;\n  if (writer === 'sed') {\n    return args.some((word) => word === '--in-place' || word.startsWith('--in-place=') || SHORT_FLAG_IN_PLACE.test(word));\n  }\n  if (writer === 'perl') {\n    return args.some((word) => PERL_IN_PLACE.test(word) && !PERL_ARGUMENT_SWITCH.test(word));\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n\n// A writer is handed the words a shell would hand it, so one scan splits the command at\n// the separators a shell runs and keeps every quote and backslash in its word. A\n// separator or a space inside quotes is text, a backslash outside quotes carries the\n// character after it, and a `#` that opens a word runs to the end of its line. Text that\n// ends inside a quote cannot be split this way, so it returns null and the caller reads\n// it by the plain split.\nfunction shellSegments(text) {\n  const segments = [];\n  let words = [];\n  let word = '';\n  let quoted = null;\n  let escaped = false;\n\n  const endWord = () => {\n    if (word !== '') words.push(word);\n    word = '';\n  };\n  const endSegment = () => {\n    endWord();\n    if (words.length > 0) segments.push(words);\n    words = [];\n  };\n\n  for (let index = 0; index < text.length; index += 1) {\n    const char = text[index];\n    if (quoted !== null) {\n      word += char;\n      if (char === '\\\\' && escaped) {\n        word += text[index + 1] ?? '';\n        index += 1;\n      } else if (char === quoted) {\n        quoted = null;\n      }\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '#' && word === '') {\n      while (index + 1 < text.length && text[index + 1] !== '\\n') index += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '\\\\') {\n      // A backslash before a newline joins the two lines, and every other one carries the\n      // character after it so a quote it protects cannot open a quoted run.\n      if (text[index + 1] === '\\n') {\n        index += 1;\n        continue;\n      }\n      if (text[index + 1] === '\\r' && text[index + 2] === '\\n') {\n        index += 2;\n        continue;\n      }\n      word += char + (text[index + 1] ?? '');\n      index += 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (char === '\"' || char === \"'\") {\n      quoted = char;\n      // A double quote and a quote opened right after `$` take a backslash escape, while\n      // a plain single quote keeps every character up to its closer.\n      escaped = char === '\"' || text[index - 1] === '$';\n      word += char;\n      continue;\n    }\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '780,900p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794833222397,"end":1794833223959}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794833223968,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e4658d91001m5dIVBt0FDoHBw","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e464e2920012UpPoZU3Umm7kV","type":"tool","id":"call_a1b6bd3d8f9a400dbdb1718c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && grep -rn \"autopilot\" .skilled/commands/speckit/*.md .skilled/commands/deep/*.md 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \"=== unattended ===\"; grep -rn \":unattended\" .skilled/commands/speckit/*.md | head -10"},"output":".skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:2:description: \"End-to-end SpecKit workflow, 13 steps. Modes :auto, :confirm, :autopilot; :with-* workflow modifiers.\"\n.skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:3:argument-hint: \"<feature-description> [:auto|:confirm|:autopilot|:unattended|--unattended] [:with-research] [:with-context] [:with-phases]\"\n.skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:32:1. Parse `$ARGUMENTS` for `:auto`, `:confirm`, `:autopilot`, `:unattended`, or `--unattended`.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:36:5. For `:autopilot`, `:unattended`, or `--unattended`, bind execution mode to `autopilot`; do not alias it to `:auto`.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:63:| `:autopilot`, `:unattended`, or `--unattended` | `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` with `execution_mode` autopilot and `unattended_autopilot` enabled |\n.skilled/commands/speckit/complete.md:68:`:autopilot`, `:unattended`, and `--unattended` are a branch-preserved execution envelope, not a quieter spelling of `:auto`.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/implement.md:2:description: \"Implementation workflow, 9 steps: execute pre-planned work from plan.md. Modes :auto, :confirm, :autopilot.\"\n.skilled/commands/speckit/implement.md:3:argument-hint: \"<spec-folder> [:auto|:confirm|:autopilot|:unattended|--unattended] [--phase-folder=PATH]\"\n.skilled/command