{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794833536035,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"id":"prt_1e46a5423001fJXJ1VT7a70R4j","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e46a4cce0019hdVt3t4CZI4W6","type":"step-start","snapshot":"db967df823979e4548e12f02210470b7ac8f089d"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794833540906,"sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e46a6488001xIXcZj7biw8QE3","sessionID":"ses_e1b9f9c06ffemmJMnzkzBHTwnQ","messageID":"msg_1e46a4cce0019hdVt3t4CZI4W6","type":"tool","id":"call_0091dde903ff4b7489fa920c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1,80p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md"},"output":"---\nname: sk-code-review\ndescription: \"Stack-agnostic code-review for sk-code: findings-first severity, security/correctness minimums, and surface evidence.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Write]\nversion: 1.9.18.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n---\n\n# sk-code-review Mode - Stack-Agnostic Findings-First Review\n\nUniversal findings-first review baseline paired with `sk-code` surface standards evidence for the detected code surface.\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\n### Activation Triggers\n\nUse the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) when:\n- A user asks for code review, PR review, or merge readiness.\n- A workflow dispatches `@review` for pre-commit or gate validation.\n- A user requests security/correctness risk analysis before merge.\n- A user wants severity-ranked findings with file:line evidence.\n\n### Keyword Triggers\n\n`review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues`, `merge readiness`\n\n### Use Cases\n\n1. Review-only pass: findings-first output with no code edits.\n2. Gate validation: a pass or fail that follows severity for orchestrated workflows, with any score advisory only.\n3. Focused risk pass: security, concurrency, correctness, or removal-focused review.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n\n- Feature implementation without review intent; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n- Pure documentation editing where code behavior is not being assessed.\n- Git-only workflow tasks (branching, rebasing, commit hygiene) without code quality evaluation intent.\n- Applying review fixes after findings are accepted; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n- Author-side quality gates before review; use `sk-code-quality`.\n- Root-cause debugging; use the surface's `workflow-debug.md` doctrine.\n- Verification evidence collection; use the surface's `workflow-verify.md` doctrine.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this mode for a code review, a pull-request review, merge readiness or a security and correctness pass over a diff.\nIts vocabulary is `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues` and `merge readiness`.\nThe first resource it loads is `references/review-core.md`, the doctrine both `@review` and `@deep-review` read.\n\nReview keeps no detection of its own.\nThe quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver, and it applies the shared detection contract, [`stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) section 2.\nA surface the hub's router bundles from request words is only a candidate: it applies to the files the resolver places in it.\n\nThe rule-override algorithm it hands to the review lives in [`review-core.md`](./references/review-core.md) section 9.\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Phase 1: Scope and Baseline\n\n1. Check the two efficiency gates in section 8 before scoping anything: M-1 skips a review whose pull request is unchanged since the last one, and M-2 skips a diff below its opt-in evidence threshold unless it touches a sensitive path. A skip ends the review there, with the status line alone.\n2. Inspect the review target (`git diff`, staged diff, file list, or commit range).\n3. Load baseline standards from the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family).\n4. Resolve the rule sources for the reviewed files in one call, from the reviewed repository's root, where `<hub>` is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from (`.skilled/skills/sk-code` in the repository that ships it):\n\n   ```bash\n   git diff --name-only --diff-filter=d <range>   # the reviewed paths that still exist\n   node <hub>/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <file> [<file>...]\n   ```\n\n   Pass only paths that exist as regular files. One deleted or missing path makes the runner stop the whole call with `FILE_NOT_FOUND`, exit 3, which would drop every surface and override for the files that do exist.\n\n   - For a diff, `--diff-filter=d` leaves the deleted paths out.\n   - For a file list, drop any path that is not a regular file.\n   - List a deleted path on the `Not checked:` line when its removal matters to the review, such as a deleted test or a removed caller.\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: sk-code-review\ndescription: \"Stack-agnostic code-review for sk-code: findings-first severity, security/correctness minimums, and surface evidence.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Write]\nversion: 1.9.18.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n---\n\n# sk-code-review Mode - Stack-Agnostic Findings-First Review\n\nUniversal findings-first review baseline paired with `sk-code` surface standards evidence for the detected code surface.\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\n### Activation Triggers\n\nUse the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family) when:\n- A user asks for code review, PR review, or merge readiness.\n- A workflow dispatches `@review` for pre-commit or gate validation.\n- A user requests security/correctness risk analysis before merge.\n- A user wants severity-ranked findings with file:line evidence.\n\n### Keyword Triggers\n\n`review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues`, `merge readiness`\n\n### Use Cases\n\n1. Review-only pass: findings-first output with no code edits.\n2. Gate validation: a pass or fail that follows severity for orchestrated workflows, with any score advisory only.\n3. Focused risk pass: security, concurrency, correctness, or removal-focused review.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n\n- Feature implementation without review intent; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n- Pure documentation editing where code behavior is not being assessed.\n- Git-only workflow tasks (branching, rebasing, commit hygiene) without code quality evaluation intent.\n- Applying review fixes after findings are accepted; load the surface packet (`sk-code-skilled`) that carries the implement doctrine.\n- Author-side quality gates before review; use `sk-code-quality`.\n- Root-cause debugging; use the surface's `workflow-debug.md` doctrine.\n- Verification evidence collection; use the surface's `workflow-verify.md` doctrine.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this mode for a code review, a pull-request review, merge readiness or a security and correctness pass over a diff.\nIts vocabulary is `review`, `code review`, `pr review`, `audit`, `security review`, `request changes`, `findings`, `blocking issues` and `merge readiness`.\nThe first resource it loads is `references/review-core.md`, the doctrine both `@review` and `@deep-review` read.\n\nReview keeps no detection of its own.\nThe quality runner's explain entry is the one resolver, and it applies the shared detection contract, [`stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) section 2.\nA surface the hub's router bundles from request words is only a candidate: it applies to the files the resolver places in it.\n\nThe rule-override algorithm it hands to the review lives in [`review-core.md`](./references/review-core.md) section 9.\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Phase 1: Scope and Baseline\n\n1. Check the two efficiency gates in section 8 before scoping anything: M-1 skips a review whose pull request is unchanged since the last one, and M-2 skips a diff below its opt-in evidence threshold unless it touches a sensitive path. A skip ends the review there, with the status line alone.\n2. Inspect the review target (`git diff`, staged diff, file list, or commit range).\n3. Load baseline standards from the `sk-code-review` mode (of the sk-code family).\n4. Resolve the rule sources for the reviewed files in one call, from the reviewed repository's root, where `<hub>` is the sk-code folder this mode was loaded from (`.skilled/skills/sk-code` in the repository that ships it):\n\n   ```bash\n   git diff --name-only --diff-filter=d <range>   # the reviewed paths that still exist\n   node <hub>/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <file> [<file>...]\n   ```\n\n   Pass only paths that exist as regular files. One deleted or missing path makes the runner stop the whole call with `FILE_NOT_FOUND`, exit 3, which would drop every surface and override for the files that do exist.\n\n   - For a diff, `--diff-filter=d` leaves the deleted paths out.\n   - For a file list, drop any path that is not a regular file.\n   - List a deleted path on the `Not checked:` line when its removal matters to the review, such as a deleted test or a removed caller.\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && sed -n '1,80p' .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794833540232,"end":1794833540906}}}}
